for more on their first face-to-face  meeting between President Trump and  Vladimir Putin we're joined from  Washington by Douglas Olivet he's a  former director at the National Security  Council and senior fellow at New America  Douglas good morning morning John let's  start with this sort of differing  accounts that we're hearing about  whether or not there was acceptance of  Russia's role in meddling in the  elections what's your take on this well  as in any negotiation you know you come  out you try to spin the story so we've  had both the the Russians first and then  the Secretary of State come out and give  their account of the meeting there are  only six people in the meeting the the  two principals the Secretary of State  and foreign minister and the two  translators so it's not like we have a  neutral party who can tell us what  really went on so we have to rely on  these two accounts so where do we sort  of go from here when it comes to Russian  meddling in the u.s. election well  clearly this is a very sticky wicket as  they say for the president there are a  lot of Russia Hawks both in the  Democrats but also in the president's  own party who are deeply unhappy with  what happened last fall among other  thing not to mention Ukraine and Syria  and a host of other issues on which we  have disagreements with the Russians so  the president had a very narrow way to  thread this it's not clear he could have  come up with a solution that would have  made anyone happy it short of just D  railing relations with the Russians  which he clearly did not want to do so I  suppose this was about as good as we  were going to get given the president's  clear desire not to derail relations so  as you're watching this first meeting  what is your takeaway in terms of the  bigger picture issues of what this means  in terms of resetting the relationship  between the United States and Russia and  what do you anticipate about the  relationship going further between  President Trump and Putin given what  unfolded yesterday well you just have to  watch the body language between the two  men to tell that they really want this  relationship to work you know they lean  into each other the handshakes are  genuine they clearly want this  relationship to work  the list of issues on which there are  disagreements is very very long now we  do have a new special envoy appointed  for the Ukraine issue ambassador Kurt  Volker a very experienced and so that  may give an opportunity to resolve one  set of issues but the list of our  disagreements with Russia is long you  mentioned some of the issue Syria also  on the agenda yesterday where do we  stand well there's been a evidently a  negotiation going on for some time  between the United States the Russians  with the Jordanians also working about  getting a ceasefire in southwest Syria  next to the Jordanian border this is for  Syria one of the easier places to get a  ceasefire going which is not the same  thing as saying it's easy but this has  evidently been in negotiations for some  time the announcement was made yesterday  in coordination with the bilat and  there's hope that this could provide  something an initial ceasefire that then  can move into something larger at least  in this Southwest sector all right  quickly let's move to North Korea Doug I  just want to ask you President Putin has  said that while he doesn't want to see  North Korea acquire nuclear weapons he  is also not in favor of stepping up the  sort of economic consequences on North  Korea so any common ground between the  United States and Russia that you can  detect there's very little common ground  between us and the Russians and even  more importantly with the Chinese North  Korea has been a problem for a long time  this is not you know a problem that  president from created there's been a  problem for 60 years it's been a nuclear  problem for 20 years but we're now  getting to the point where it appears  they're about to reach some type of real  capability and our number of options is  very very small we don't have partners  who really see this problem the same way  we do  Douglas Oliver thank you thanks Dana
 

